      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Plymouth in southwest England, chosen by explorer John Smith during his 1614 voyage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plymouth%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Plymouth Rock, a large boulder under the historic portico, is traditionally said to mark the Pilgrims’ first footfall, though no evidence supports the claim.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plymouth%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Pilgrim Hall Museum, founded in 1824, is the oldest continually operating museum in the United States.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plymouth%2C_Massachusetts",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Plymouth Cordage Company, founded in 1824, once became the world’s largest rope manufacturer and now houses offices, restaurants, and stores as Cordage Commerce Center.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
